Development Guidelines: OpenClaw Dashboard (Nord Edition)
1. Visual Design: Solid Nord

Since we are ditching transparency, the UI will focus on high contrast and color-coded information using the four Nord sub-palettes:

    Background (Polar Night): Use #2E3440 for the main canvas and #3B4252 for card backgrounds to create depth without blur.

    Typography (Snow Storm): Primary text in #ECEFF4, secondary in #D8DEE9.

    Accents (Frost): Use #88C0D0 (Primary Frost) for active states, buttons, and "Online" indicators.

    Status (Aurora): * Success/Health: #A3BE8C (Green)

        Warnings/Alerts: #EBCB8B (Yellow)

        Errors/Critical: #BF616A (Red)

2. Backend Architecture (Go)

The Go backend will serve as a lightweight API and file server.

    File I/O: Use os.UserHomeDir() to reliably locate ~/.openclaw across Linux and macOS.

    Concurrency: Use a time.Ticker in a goroutine to poll the directory for updates, or fsnotify for event-driven refreshes.

    Embedding: Use the embed package to bake the frontend into the Go binary:
    Go

    //go:embed ui/*
    var content embed.FS

3. Data Schema & Directory Mapping

The dashboard will map specific files in ~/.openclaw to the 10 panels:
Dashboard Panel	Target File/Resource	Data Type
System Health	/proc (Linux) or sysctl (macOS)	PID, Mem, Uptime
Alerts Banner	logs/error.log or alerts.json	String/Level
Cost Cards	data/usage.db or costs.json	Float64
Kanban Board	todo.json	Struct Array
Cron Jobs	crontabs.json	Cron Schedule
Active Sessions	sessions/active.json	Map[string]Session
Git Log	exec.Command("git", "log") in repo	String
4. Frontend Implementation (Zero Dependencies)

    CSS Grid Layout: Define a 12-column grid to handle the 10 panels across different screen sizes.

    State Management: Use a simple fetch() loop in JavaScript to grab JSON from the Go /api/status endpoint every 60 seconds.

    Transitions: Use CSS transition: background-color 0.2s ease for hover effects on cards instead of transparency.

5. Implementation Roadmap
Step 1: Core Server

Create the basic Go HTTP server that serves a "Hello World" from an embedded file and prints the contents of ~/.openclaw to the terminal.
Step 2: The Data Parser

Define the Go structs that match the OpenClaw configuration files. Implement a Refresh() function that reads these files and returns a unified DashboardState JSON.
Step 3: Nord CSS Framework

Build a style.css using CSS Variables for the Nord colors. This makes it easy to maintain the "Solid" look:
CSS

:root {
  --nord0: #2E3440; /* Darkest */
  --nord3: #4C566A; /* Lighter Grey */
  --nord8: #88C0D0; /* Frost Blue */
}
.card {
  background-color: var(--nord1);
  border: 1px solid var(--nord3);
  padding: 1rem;
}

6. Senior Dev Workflow (NixOS/Neovim)

    Nix: Add a flake.nix with devShells including go, gopls, and templ (if you want type-safe HTML).

    Neovim: Leverage nvim-lspconfig with gopls for deep static analysis of your file parsing logic.
